"""Additional independently worked packs, including one from every source paper."""
def extend(add):
 add(21,'Mathematics','Topology','Moderate','Nonempty A,B⊂R have C=A∩B. Which condition forces C to be empty?',
 ['A,B open and C compact','A,B open and C closed','A,B both dense in R','A open and B compact'],'A',
 ['In R, a compact set is closed and bounded.','An intersection of open sets is open. Use connectedness of R.'],
 r'Under A, C is open and compact, hence also closed. Since R is connected, its only clopen subsets are ∅ and R. R is not compact, so C=∅. **A**. For B and C take A=B=R, giving nonempty C. For D take A=R and B={0}, giving C={0}. These examples satisfy the specified nonemptiness.',
 r'A clopen set is both open and closed. Connectedness is equivalent to having no proper nonempty clopen subset. In R, compactness is equivalent to closedness and boundedness; it is stronger than closedness alone. The empty set is open, closed, and compact. Dense sets need not intersect (Q and its complement), but density also does not force disjointness. In a disconnected ambient space, a nonempty compact open component can exist, so the conclusion uses the particular topology of R.',
 [('If U⊂R is open and compact, what is U?','U must be empty by the same connectedness argument.'),('Can a closed bounded subset of R be nonempty and open?','No. A nonempty clopen subset of R would have to be R, which is unbounded.')],pid='2020-june-30nov')
 add(22,'Mathematics','Real Analysis','Easy',r'Find $\lim_{n\to\infty}(1+\sqrt2+\sqrt[3]3+\cdots+\sqrt[n]n)/n$.',
 ['0','1','2','Does not exist'],'B',['First find the limit of aₖ=k¹ᐟᵏ.','The expression is the arithmetic mean of the first n terms of that sequence.'],
 r'$k^{1/k}=\exp((\log k)/k)\to1$. The arithmetic means of a convergent sequence have the same limit by Cesàro’s theorem. Therefore the requested limit is 1, option **B**.',
 r'If aₖ→L, then (a₁+⋯+aₙ)/n→L. For a proof, choose K so |aₖ−L|<ε for k≥K. The finite initial error sum divided by n tends to zero, while the tail’s average error is bounded by ε. The converse is false: an alternating sequence can have convergent averages without converging itself. Taking logarithms is useful for powers kᵅ⁽ᵏ⁾; here log k grows more slowly than k.',
 [('Find the limit of (1+1/2+⋯+1/n)/n.','The sequence 1/k tends to zero; its arithmetic means also tend to zero.'),('Give a sequence whose means converge but which does not converge.',r'$a_k=(-1)^k$ has means tending to zero, although aₖ oscillates.')],pid='2021-june')
 add(21,'Mathematics','Real Analysis','Moderate',r'Let aₙ=n+n⁻¹. Find the sum $\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(-1)^{n+1}a_{n+1}/n!$.',
 ['Does not converge',r'$e^{-1}-1$',r'$e^{-1}$',r'$e^{-1}+1$'],'D',
 ['Expand aₙ₊₁=(n+1)+1/(n+1).','Use the exponential power series and shift the factorial indices.'],
 r'The series is absolutely convergent by the ratio test. Split its summand into $n/n!+1/n!+1/(n+1)!$. The signed sums of these three components are respectively $e^{-1}$, $1-e^{-1}$, and $e^{-1}$: for the first set m=n−1; for the last set m=n+1. Their total is $1+e^{-1}$. Hence **D**.',
 r'The entire exponential series eᶻ=Σzⁿ/n! converges absolutely for every complex z. Absolute convergence permits finite decomposition, index shifts, and removal of finitely many terms. Identities n/n!=1/(n−1)! and [(n+1)n!]=(n+1)! are central here. When shifting an alternating series, track the new sign and starting index explicitly. A ratio-test limit below one proves absolute convergence; it does not compute the sum by itself.',
 [(r'Find $\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(-1)^{n+1}n/n!$.',r'Put m=n−1. The result is $\sum_{m\ge0}(-1)^m/m!=e^{-1}$.'),(r'Find $\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}1/(n+1)!$.',r'Removing the n=0 and n=1 terms from e gives e−2.')],pid='2022-june')
 add(21,'Mathematics','Discrete Mathematics','Hard','S is infinite and the axiom of choice holds. Which set is necessarily in bijection with S?',
 ['Q','R','S×S','P(S)'],'C',
 ['An infinite set can be countable or have larger cardinality.','With choice, the square of any infinite cardinal equals that cardinal.'],
 r'The axiom of choice allows S to be well ordered, with infinite cardinal κ. The infinite-cardinal multiplication theorem gives κ·κ=κ, so S and S×S are in bijection. **C**. A fails for S=R; B fails for S=N. D always fails by Cantor’s theorem: no set is in bijection with its own power set.',
 r'Cardinality compares sets through bijections. Under choice, every infinite cardinal κ satisfies κ+κ=κ and κ·κ=κ. One proof of the square theorem orders ordinal pairs first by max coordinate and then lexicographically. A transfinite induction on infinite initial ordinals shows each proper initial segment of this order has cardinality below κ; assigning pairs their ranks gives an injection κ×κ→κ. The reverse injection is α↦(α,0), and Cantor–Schröder–Bernstein yields equality. Choice is relevant for arbitrary infinite sets; finite sets instead have |S×S|=|S|². Cantor’s diagonal argument proves |P(S)|>|S| regardless of choice.',
 [('Give an enumeration of N×N.','List pairs by increasing sum of coordinates. Every diagonal is finite and every pair occurs on one diagonal.'),('For a set S with three elements, is S×S in bijection with S?','No. Their sizes are 9 and 3.')],pid='2023-june')
 add(21,'Mathematics','Real Analysis','Easy',r'Find inf U for $U=\{x\in\mathbb R:x^2-9x+18\le0,\ x^2-7x+12\le0\}$.',
 ['5','4','3','2'],'C',['Factor each quadratic.','Both inequalities must hold, so intersect their solution intervals.'],
 r'The first inequality is (x−3)(x−6)≤0, giving [3,6]. The second is (x−3)(x−4)≤0, giving [3,4]. Their intersection is U=[3,4], whose infimum and minimum are 3. **C**.',
 r'For a quadratic with positive leading coefficient and real roots a≤b, its value is nonpositive on [a,b]. A pair of simultaneous conditions means intersection, while an “or” condition means union. The infimum is the greatest lower bound and need not belong to the set; a minimum must belong. Here non-strict inequalities include both endpoints. Repeated roots and negative leading coefficients change the sign pattern, so use a sign chart when needed.',
 [('Find inf{x:(x−1)(x−5)<0}.','The set is (1,5), so its infimum is 1, but it has no minimum.'),('Intersect [−2,3] and [1,7].','The intersection is [1,3], with infimum 1 and supremum 3.')],pid='2023-december')
 add(21,'Mathematics','Real Analysis','Moderate',r'For $A=\{x\in\mathbb Q:0<(\sqrt2-1)x<\sqrt2+1\}$, considered in R, which statement holds?',
 [r'$\sup A=2+2\sqrt3$',r'$\sup A=3+2\sqrt2$',r'$\inf A=2+2\sqrt3$',r'$\inf A=3+2\sqrt2$'],'B',
 ['Divide by the positive coefficient √2−1.','Rational numbers are dense in every real interval.'],
 r'The inequality gives $0<x<(\sqrt2+1)/(\sqrt2-1)=(\sqrt2+1)^2=3+2\sqrt2$. Rational density implies every smaller real number fails to be an upper bound: there is a rational in the interval above it and below the endpoint. Thus sup A=3+2√2, **B**. Similarly inf A=0. Neither bound belongs to A.',
 r'Density of Q in R means every open interval contains a rational. It allows a set of rational points to have irrational supremum in R. An upper bound b is the supremum if every b−ε is exceeded by an element of the set. Rationalizing a denominator uses multiplication by its conjugate; here (√2−1)(√2+1)=1. Bounds depend on the ambient ordered field: Q is not order complete, so an irrational least upper bound need not exist within Q.',
 [('What is sup(Q∩(0,√3)) as a subset of R?',r'It is √3; there is no maximum.'),('Does that set have a supremum in Q?','No. Every rational upper bound exceeds √3 and can be replaced by a smaller rational upper bound.')],pid='2024-june')
 add(21,'Mathematics','Discrete Mathematics','Easy','How many injective functions are there from {1,…,7} to {1,…,10}?',
 [r'$10^7$',r'$10!/7!$',r'$10!/3!$',r'$7^{10}$'],'C',
 ['An injection cannot reuse an image.','Count choices successively for the seven labelled domain elements.'],
 r'There are 10 choices for the first image, then 9,8,7,6,5,4 for the remaining images. Multiplying gives 10·9·8·7·6·5·4=10!/3!=604800. **C**.',
 r'For a labelled domain of size m and codomain of size n, injections number n!/(n−m)! when m≤n, and zero otherwise. Unrestricted functions number nᵐ. Choosing only the image subset gives binomial(n,m), but each such subset supports m! bijections from the domain, recovering the injection count. Surjections require a different calculation, commonly inclusion–exclusion.',
 [('Count injections from a 3-element set to a 5-element set.','5·4·3=60.'),('Count unrestricted maps from the same sets.','Each of the three arguments has five independent choices, so 5³=125.')],pid='2024-december-28feb')
 add(21,'Mathematics','Topology','Hard',r'For nonempty A,B⊂R, let S=C(A,R), T=C(B,R), U=C(A×A,R). Which claim is true?',
 ['If A is finite, S and U are in bijection','If A is finite and B=[0,1], S and T are not in bijection','S and U are never in bijection','If A≠B, T and U are not in bijection'],'A',
 ['A finite subset of R has the discrete subspace topology.','Compare cardinalities of finite real products and of C([0,1],R).'],
 r'If |A|=m≥1 is finite, every map on A and A×A is continuous, so S has size |Rᵐ| and U has size |Rᵐ²|. Both equal the continuum; hence **A**. For B, C([0,1],R) also has continuum cardinality: constants give a lower bound, while restriction to the countable dense rational subset is injective and gives an upper bound |Rᴺ|=continuum. Thus B is false. C is contradicted by finite A. For D choose A={0}, B={0,1}; U≅R and T≅R² are in bijection although A≠B.',
 r'Cardinality is distinct from topology and vector-space dimension. All nonempty finite Cartesian powers of R have continuum size even when their Euclidean dimensions differ. On a finite subset of a metric space, every singleton is open and all maps are continuous. A continuous real-valued function on a space is determined by its values on a dense subset: limits from that subset fix every remaining value. A countable determining set bounds the cardinality by |Rᴺ|=(2^ℵ₀)^ℵ₀=2^ℵ₀. Set-theoretic bijections here need not be continuous as maps between function spaces.',
 [('How many continuous real-valued functions are there on a 2-point discrete space?','They are determined by two arbitrary real values, so their cardinality is the continuum.'),('Why is restriction to a dense subset injective for continuous functions?','If two continuous functions agree on the dense subset, their difference is zero there and, by continuity, zero everywhere.')],pid='2024-december-02mar')
 add(21,'Mathematics','Discrete Mathematics','Easy','Nonnegative integers p,q satisfy statements (I): p+k=q for some integer k≥1; (II): q+k=p for some integer k≥1. Which claim holds?',
 ['Both can be true','Both are false iff p=q','For every p,q at least one is true','Some p≠q make both false'],'B',
 ['Statement I is equivalent to q>p.','Use trichotomy of the integer order.'],
 r'I holds exactly when q>p, by taking k=q−p. II holds exactly when p>q. They cannot both hold. When p=q neither admits a positive k, and when p≠q exactly one holds. Therefore **B** is the unique true claim.',
 r'A linear order satisfies trichotomy: exactly one of p<q, p=q, p>q holds. Integer discreteness means strict inequality is equivalent to a difference of at least one. The condition k≥1 matters; allowing k=0 would make both statements true at equality. Translate existential arithmetic statements into simple inequalities before assessing universal claims. A single counterexample suffices to refute a universal statement.',
 [('Which statements hold at p=2,q=5?','I holds with k=3; II is false.'),('If k≥0 were allowed, what happens at p=q?','Both statements hold with k=0.')],pid='2025-june')
 add(21,'Statistics','Statistical Inference','Moderate',r'X∼Binomial(n,p), with n∈{5,6}, p∈{1/4,3/4}. Given X=3, find the maximum likelihood estimate of (n,p).',
 [r'$(5,1/4)$',r'$(5,3/4)$',r'$(6,3/4)$',r'$(6,1/4)$'],'B',
 ['There are only four admissible parameter pairs.','Evaluate binomial(n,3)p³(1−p)ⁿ⁻³ at each pair; n is also unknown.'],
 r'The four likelihoods in option order are 90/1024, 270/1024, 135/1024, 135/1024. The unique maximum is 270/1024 at (5,3/4). Hence **B**. For n=6, three successes and three failures make the two p choices equally likely.',
 r'Maximum likelihood chooses the admissible parameter maximising the probability of the observed data. In a discrete finite parameter space, direct enumeration is exact and often simpler than calculus. For binomial data the likelihood includes the combinatorial coefficient binomial(n,x); it cannot be dropped when n varies. The unrestricted formula p̂=x/n assumes n fixed and a continuous parameter p∈[0,1]; it does not solve this restricted joint problem. Equal likelihoods can produce nonunique MLEs, so compare all admissible pairs.',
 [('With n=5 fixed and p restricted to {1/4,3/4}, which p is preferred if X=1?',r'The likelihood ratio for p=3/4 over p=1/4 is 3^(2x−n)=3^(−3)<1, so p=1/4.'),('If n=6 is fixed and X=3, is the restricted MLE unique?','No. Both p=1/4 and p=3/4 have equal likelihood.')],pid='2025-december')
 add(21,'Statistics','Statistical Inference','Moderate',r'Independent Xᵢ are exponential with means 1/(iθ), i=1,2,3, θ>0. Which displayed statistic is sufficient for θ?',
 [r'$3X_1+3X_2+3X_3$',r'$X_1+2X_2+3X_3$',r'$3X_1+2X_2+X_3$',r'$X_1+X_2/2+X_3/3$'],'B',
 ['An exponential variable with mean 1/(iθ) has rate iθ.','Multiply the three densities and inspect where θ and the data interact.'],
 r'The joint density is $6\theta^3\exp[-\theta(x_1+2x_2+3x_3)]\mathbf1_{x_i\ge0}$. By the factorization theorem, T=X₁+2X₂+3X₃ is sufficient for θ, so **B**. The likelihood ratio of two sample points is parameter-independent precisely when their weighted sums agree; this also identifies T as minimal sufficient. The other weight vectors are not proportional to (1,2,3) and do not determine T on the positive orthant.',
 r'For independent exponentials with rates cᵢθ and known positive constants cᵢ, the density factors through T=ΣcᵢXᵢ. Each cᵢXᵢ has exponential rate θ, so T has Gamma(shape n, rate θ). Its mean is n/θ. Distinguish rate from scale or mean when converting a statement into a density. Any one-to-one transformation of a sufficient statistic remains sufficient; arbitrary reweighting need not. Sufficiency describes information retained by a statistic, rather than whether that statistic itself estimates the parameter without bias.',
 [('For means 1/θ and 1/(4θ), give a sufficient statistic.',r'T=X₁+4X₂; its distribution is Gamma(2, rate θ).'),('For the original three observations, find the likelihood MLE of θ.',r'The log likelihood is constant+3log θ−θT, giving θ̂=3/T for T>0.')],pid='2026-june')
 add(31,'Mathematics','Linear Algebra','Easy',r'For which real a,b does $p(x,y)=a^2x_1y_1+abx_2y_1+abx_1y_2+b^2x_2y_2$ define an inner product on R²?',
 ['a>0,b>0','ab>0','a=b=0','For no a,b'],'D',
 ['Factor p(x,x).','Find a nonzero vector at which that square vanishes.'],
 r'$p(x,x)=(ax_1+bx_2)^2$. If (a,b)≠(0,0), the nonzero vector (b,−a) has p(x,x)=0. If a=b=0, every vector has zero squared length. Thus positive definiteness fails in all cases, and **D** holds.',
 r'A real inner product is a symmetric bilinear form positive on every nonzero vector. A square of one linear functional is only positive semidefinite in a space of dimension at least two because that functional has a nontrivial kernel. Its matrix vvᵀ has rank at most one. For a symmetric matrix, an inner product requires all eigenvalues positive, equivalently positive definiteness. Nonnegativity alone defines a degenerate form, not an inner product.',
 [('Does x₁y₁+x₂y₂ define an inner product on R²?','Yes. Its quadratic form is x₁²+x₂²>0 for every nonzero vector.'),('What is the kernel of the form for a=2,b=3?',r'The vectors with 2x₁+3x₂=0, a line spanned by (3,−2).')])
 add(32,'Mathematics','Linear Algebra','Easy','Which quadratic form on R² is positive definite?',
 [r'XY',r'X²−XY+Y²',r'X²+2XY+Y²',r'X²+XY'],'B',
 ['Complete the square in B.','For other choices, look for a nonzero zero or a negative value.'],
 r'$X^2-XY+Y^2=(X-Y/2)^2+3Y^2/4$, strictly positive for every nonzero pair, so **B**. A is negative at (1,−1). C=(X+Y)² vanishes at (1,−1). D vanishes at (0,1) and is negative at (1,−2).',
 r'For Q=aX²+2bXY+cY², the symmetric matrix is [[a,b],[b,c]]. Sylvester’s criterion in dimension two gives positive definiteness exactly when a>0 and ac−b²>0. The coefficient of XY is 2b, not b. Positive semidefinite forms may vanish on nonzero vectors. Completing squares gives a direct proof and often identifies the null directions or negative directions.',
 [('For what t is X²+tXY+Y² positive definite?',r'The determinant condition is 1−t²/4>0, hence |t|<2.'),('What happens at t=2?','It is (X+Y)², positive semidefinite but not positive definite.')])
 add(33,'Mathematics','Complex Analysis','Moderate',r'For the positively oriented circle |z−1|=1, find $(2\pi i)^{-1}\oint dz/(z^3-1)$.',
 ['3','1/3','2','1/2'],'B',
 ['Locate all three cube roots of unity relative to the circle.','For a simple zero a of h, the residue of 1/h at a is 1/h′(a).'],
 r'The poles are 1 and ω,ω² with ω=e^(2πi/3). The distances of ω and ω² from the centre 1 are √3>1, so only z=1 is inside. Its residue is 1/(3·1²)=1/3. The residue theorem makes the normalised integral equal to that residue. **B**.',
 r'The residue theorem gives a positively oriented contour integral as 2πi times the sum of enclosed residues, weighted by winding numbers for general contours. A simple pole g(z)/h(z) with h(a)=0 and h′(a)≠0 has residue g(a)/h′(a). Poles outside the contour contribute nothing, and a pole on the contour invalidates the ordinary formula. Check geometric location before calculating residues; the contour centre need not be zero.',
 [(r'Find $(2\pi i)^{-1}\oint_{|z-1|=1/2} dz/(z^3-1)$.','Again only pole 1 is enclosed; the answer is 1/3.'),(r'Find the same normalised integral around |z|=2.',r'All three residues sum to (1+ω+ω²)/3=0, so the integral is zero.')])
 add(34,'Mathematics','Complex Analysis','Hard',r'For which positive integers p does f(z)=sin z/zᵖ have a holomorphic primitive on C\{0}?',
 ['All even integers','All odd integers','All multiples of 3','All multiples of 4'],'B',
 ['A primitive on the punctured plane exists precisely when the residue at zero vanishes.','Find which term of the sine expansion could produce z⁻¹.'],
 r'The Laurent expansion is $\sum_{k\ge0}(-1)^k z^{2k+1-p}/(2k+1)!$. A z⁻¹ term requires 2k+1−p=−1, or p=2k+2, an even positive integer. Its coefficient is then nonzero, preventing a primitive. For odd p there is no z⁻¹ term and every Laurent term can be integrated to a single-valued holomorphic function on the punctured plane. Thus **B**.',
 r'A holomorphic function has a global primitive exactly when all closed-curve integrals vanish. The punctured plane is not simply connected, and its loop periods are controlled by the residue at the missing point. Integrating z⁻¹ gives a logarithm with nonzero loop period; all other integer Laurent powers have single-valued power primitives. Holomorphicity alone on a multiply connected domain does not ensure a primitive. The residue obstruction is necessary and sufficient here because there is only one puncture and no other singularity.',
 [('Does 1/z² have a primitive on the punctured plane?',r'Yes: −1/z is a single-valued holomorphic primitive.'),('Does sin z/z² have a primitive there?','No. Its Laurent series begins 1/z−z/6+⋯, with residue 1.')])
 add(35,'Mathematics','Complex Analysis','Moderate',r'For the positively oriented circle |z−1|=1/2, compute $\oint e^{1/z}/(z^2-1)\,dz$.',
 [r'$i\pi e$',r'$-i\pi e$',r'$\pi e$',r'$-\pi e$'],'A',
 ['Check whether 0,1,−1 lie inside the contour.','Only the simple pole at 1 is enclosed.'],
 r'The disc is centred at 1 with radius 1/2. It contains z=1 but neither 0 (distance 1) nor −1 (distance 2). The residue at 1 is e/(1+1)=e/2. Therefore the integral is 2πi·e/2=iπe. **A**.',
 r'An essential singularity outside a contour does not affect the residue sum inside it. Here e^(1/z) is holomorphic throughout the closed disc except that the quotient has a simple pole at 1. For a factorised denominator (z−a)h(z), the simple-pole residue is numerator(a)/h(a). Positive versus negative orientation changes the sign. Determine the enclosed singularities before applying Cauchy’s formula or the residue theorem.',
 [(r'Compute the same integral around |z+1|=1/2.',r'Only −1 is enclosed, with residue e^(−1)/(−2). The integral is −iπ/e.'),('What changes if the original circle is traversed clockwise?','The integral becomes −iπe.')])
 add(37,'Mathematics','Number Theory','Easy','Which claim is true for the stated integers?',
 ['Every even n≥16 divides (n−1)!+3','Every odd n≥16 divides (n−1)!','Every even n≥16 divides (n−1)!','For even n≥16, n² divides n!+1'],'C',
 ['For even n, write n=2m.','Both distinct factors 2 and m occur in (n−1)! when m≥8.'],
 r'For even n≥16, the distinct numbers 2 and n/2 appear among 1,…,n−1 and their product is n. Hence n divides (n−1)!, proving **C**. A then has remainder 3 modulo n. B fails at n=17, because none of 1,…,16 contains a factor 17. D fails since n divides n! and therefore n!+1 has remainder 1 even modulo n.',
 r'To prove divisibility by a composite number, locate enough prime factors or identify factors whose product equals that number within a larger product. Distinct-factor reasoning must be checked: for n=4 the two proposed factors 2 and n/2 coincide, and 4 does not divide 3!. Universal claims are disproved by a single valid counterexample. Congruences reduce adding small constants to a factorial once its divisibility is known.',
 [('Does every even n≥6 divide (n−1)!?','Yes: 2 and n/2 are distinct and both occur; the same proof applies.'),('Why does a prime p fail to divide (p−1)!?','A prime dividing a product must divide one factor, but all factors are positive and below p.')])
 add(38,'Mathematics','Abstract Algebra','Easy','For a nonempty set X, consider intersection and symmetric difference as operations on P(X). Which gives a group?',
 ['Both operations','Intersection only','Symmetric difference only','Neither'],'C',
 ['A△A=∅, and A△∅=A.','Under intersection, the identity must be X. Can a proper subset have an inverse?'],
 r'Symmetric difference is associative and commutative, has identity ∅, and every set is its own inverse. Thus it is an abelian group. Intersection has identity X, but ∅ cannot have an inverse because ∅∩B=∅≠X for every B. X is nonempty, so intersection is not a group. **C**.',
 r'A group requires closure, associativity, an identity, and an inverse for every element. Identify a subset with its 0–1 characteristic function. Symmetric difference becomes coordinatewise addition modulo 2, proving associativity and self-inverse behavior. Intersection becomes coordinatewise multiplication; only the all-one function X is invertible. Together these operations form a Boolean ring, a useful connection between set algebra and algebra over the two-element field.',
 [('Find {1,2}△{2,3}.','The result is {1,3}; elements present in exactly one set remain.'),('In this group, solve A△B=C for B.','Apply △A to both sides. Since A△A=∅, the unique solution is B=A△C.')])
 add(39,'Mathematics','Number Theory','Hard','Euler’s totient φ(n) counts integers 1≤a≤n coprime to n. Which statement is NOT true?',
 ['Infinitely many n satisfy φ(n)>φ(n+1)','Infinitely many n satisfy φ(n)<φ(n+1)','Some N>2 satisfies φ(N)<φ(n) for every n>N','The set {φ(n)/n:n∈N} has finitely many limit points'],'D',
 ['Use large odd primes to establish rises and falls.','For a fixed prime p, consider φ(pq)/(pq) with varying primes q.'],
 r'For an odd prime p>3, φ(p)=p−1 while φ(p+1)≤(p+1)/2, so A holds at infinitely many n=p. Also φ(p−1)≤(p−1)/2<p−1=φ(p), proving B at n=p−1. For C choose N=6: φ(6)=2, and φ(n)>2 for n>6. Indeed φ(n) is even for n>2, and φ(n)=2 only for n=3,4,6. For D fix any prime p and let distinct primes q≠p tend to infinity. The distinct ratios φ(pq)/(pq)=(1−1/p)(1−1/q) tend to 1−1/p. Different p give infinitely many distinct limit points, so **D** is false.',
 r'Inclusion–exclusion on prime divisors gives φ(n)=n∏ₚ∣ₙ(1−1/p). For even n this is at most n/2. For n>2, residues coprime to n pair as a and n−a without a fixed point, so φ(n) is even. To classify φ(n)=2, each prime divisor p contributes p−1 dividing φ(n), so p is 2 or 3; using φ(2ᵃ3ᵇ) restricts the possibilities to 3,4,6. A limit point of a set requires distinct points approaching it: merely repeating φ(pᵏ)/pᵏ would not suffice. Varying a second prime supplies distinct nearby ratios.',
 [('Compute φ(12).',r'$12(1-1/2)(1-1/3)=4$.'),('Show 1 is a limit point of the ratio set.',r'For primes p→∞, the distinct ratios φ(p)/p=1−1/p approach 1.')])
 add(40,'Mathematics','Topology','Hard','A map is closed when it sends every closed set to a closed set. Which claim is true for metric spaces?',
 ['Every subspace inclusion is closed','Every product projection onto X is closed','For continuous f:X→Y and g:Y→Z, if g∘f is closed then g restricted to f(X) is closed','If f sends closed balls to closed sets then f is closed'],'C',
 ['For C, take a relatively closed F⊂f(X) and pull it back under f.','Its image under g is exactly the image of f⁻¹(F) under g∘f.'],
 r'For a closed F in the subspace f(X), continuity of f makes f⁻¹(F) closed in X. Its image under the closed map g∘f is g(F), so g|f(X) is closed. **C**. A fails for (0,1)↪R: the whole subspace is closed in itself but not in R. B fails for the closed hyperbola xy=1 in R², whose projection is R\{0}. D fails for arctan:R→R: images of closed balls are compact intervals, but its whole-domain image is (−π/2,π/2), not closed.',
 r'Continuity is defined by closed-set preimages, while closedness concerns images. A relatively closed subset F of a subspace S has the form H∩S for H closed in the ambient space. Product projections are continuous and open, but need not be closed when the other factor is noncompact; a compact other factor does ensure closedness. In R, closed balls are compact, so every continuous map sends them to compact, hence closed, images in a Hausdorff target. This restricted property cannot control arbitrary unbounded closed sets.',
 [('Why is the hyperbola xy=1 closed in R²?','It is the inverse image of the closed singleton {1} under the continuous multiplication map (x,y)↦xy.'),('Is a continuous map from a compact space to a Hausdorff space closed?','Yes. Closed subsets of the compact domain are compact; their continuous images are compact and hence closed.')])
